public interface FeatureFlagClient {
    boolean isEnabled(String flag, Context ctx);
}
```

A single method, synchronous, no exception in its signature — the client is designed to never fail loudly; a flag it can't confidently evaluate returns its configured default instead.

```java
public class CachedFeatureFlagClient implements FeatureFlagClient {
    private volatile Map<String, FlagDefinition> cache = Map.of();

    void refresh() {
        try {
            cache = backend.fetchAllFlags();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            log.warn("flag refresh failed, using stale cache", e);
        }
    }

    public boolean isEnabled(String flag, Context ctx) {
        FlagDefinition def = cache.getOrDefault(flag, FlagDefinition.disabledByDefault());
        return def.evaluate(ctx);
    }
}
```

A failed refresh logs and keeps the existing cache rather than clearing it — a stale-but-recent flag value is almost always better for the caller than suddenly falling back to every flag's hardcoded default.
